efine the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_r:\\; y-(k+1-r)=r\\bigl(x-r\\bigr). \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_r\\) is the positive integer \\(r\\); therefore every \\(S_r\\) is sunny (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor undefined).  The line \\(S_r\\) passes through the lattice point  \n\n   \\[\n   P_r=(r,\\;k+1-r). \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   **Lemma 3 (covering of \\(\\Delta_k\\)).**  \n   The family \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covers every point of \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Let \\((i,j)\\in\\Delta_k\\).  Because \\(j\\le k+1-i\\) we can write  \n\n   \\[\n   j=k+1-i-t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,i-1\\}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed \\(t=k+1-j\\) and the inequality \\(j\\ge 1\\) gives \\(t\\le i-1\\).)  \n   Consider the line \\(S_{i-t}\\) (its index lies in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) because\n   \\(1\\le i-t\\le i\\le k\\)).  Its equation, obtained from (7) with \\(r=i-t\\), is  \n\n   \\[\n   y-\\bigl(k+1-(i-t)\\bigr)=(i-t)\\bigl(x-(i-t)\\bigr). \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   y-\\bigl(k+1-i+t\\bigr)=(i-t)\\,t . \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (9) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y-\\bigl(k+1-i+t\\bigr)=\\bigl(k+1-i-t\\bigr)-\\bigl(k+1-i+t\\bigr)=-(i-t)\\,t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the right\u2011hand side of (11) equals the left\u2011hand side, so \\((i,j)\\)\n   satisfies (10) and therefore lies on \\(S_{i-t}\\).  \u220e\n\n   Translating the whole picture back by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\) shows that the\n   lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) also cover every point of the original triangle\n   \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4.*  **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and the\n   \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\); consequently the total number\n   of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f5.*  **Distinctness.**  \n   The slopes of the sunny lines are the distinct positive integers\n   \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\); the vertical lines have undefined slope, so no two of the\n   \\(n\\) lines coincide.  Thus we indeed have \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  The\n   extreme cases \\(k=0\\) (all lines vertical) and \\(k=n\\) (no vertical line,\n   only the sunny family) are obtained as specialisations of the same\n   formulas.\n\nCombining the necessary condition (1) with the explicit construction for every\nadmissible \\(k\\) yields the classification stated at the beginning.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and\nundefined (vertical).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (necessary condition)\n\nIf \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition, then the number\n\\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  By definition \\(k\\ge 0\\).  There are only \\(n\\) lines in total, so\n\\(k\\le n\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nexactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Fix such a \\(k\\).\n\n1. **Vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines.**  \n   For \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are not sunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with\n   \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).\n\n2. **Remaining triangle.**  \n   The points not yet covered form  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\;a\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   Write \\(a=n-k+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\); then \\(b\\le k+1-i\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(n-k+i,b)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\}.\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   Translating by \\((-\\,n+k,0)\\) we obtain the congruent triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\}.\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_k\\).**  \n   For each \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_r:\\; y-(k+1-r)=r\\bigl(x-r\\bigr).\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_r\\) is the positive integer \\(r\\); therefore every\n   \\(S_r\\) is sunny.  The line \\(S_r\\) passes through the lattice point\n   \\((r,k+1-r)\\).\n\n   *Claim.*  \\(\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\) covers \\(\\Delta_k\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((i,j)\\in\\Delta_k\\).  Because \\(j\\le k+1-i\\) we can write  \n\n   \\[\n   j=k+1-i-t\\qquad\\text{with }t\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,i-1\\}.\n   \\tag{18}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed \\(t=k+1-j\\) and the inequality \\(j\\ge 1\\) gives \\(t\\le i-1\\).)  \n   Consider the line \\(S_{i-t}\\); its index satisfies \\(1\\le i-t\\le i\\le k\\),\n   so the line is defined.  Its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   y-\\bigl(k+1-(i-t)\\bigr)=(i-t)\\bigl(x-(i-t)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{19}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=i\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y-\\bigl(k+1-i+t\\bigr)=(i-t)\\,t .\n   \\tag{20}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (18) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y-\\bigl(k+1-i+t\\bigr)=\\bigl(k+1-i-t\\bigr)-\\bigl(k+1-i+t\\bigr)=-(i-t)\\,t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the left\u2011hand side of (20) equals the right\u2011hand side, so\n   \\((i,j)\\) satisfies (19) and therefore lies on \\(S_{i-t}\\).  \u220e\n\n   Translating the picture back by \\((n-k,0)\\) shows that the lines\n   \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) also cover every point of the original triangle\n   \\(T_k\\).\n\n4. **Counting sunny lines.**  \n   The construction uses exactly the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) and\n   the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\); consequently the total\n   number of sunny lines is \\(k\\).\n\n5. **Di